CUTOVER (required, not optional): organization.mjs's own `classifyVuePager` already
partially decorates this exact pager (sets `aria-hidden="true"`/`tabindex="-1"` on the
current-page anchor) and organization.css already sets `display:flex` on it at
higher cascade specificity ((1,6,0) vs dashboard-pagination.css's (1,1,0) on the same
element) — both target the identical `#dashboard-repo-list .ui.borderless.pagination.
menu`. Applying only the imports/calls above without this section leaves TWO
decorators on one pager: organization.css's `display:flex` silently wins over
dashboard-pagination.css's `display:grid` (breaking the whole grid/centering/narrow-
viewport design on org dashboards specifically — the stated primary target), AND the
current-page anchor ends up `aria-hidden="true"` (from organization.mjs, applied
first in enhance() order) with `aria-current="page"` also set (from
dashboard-pagination.mjs) — a contradictory, inaccessible combination, since an
aria-hidden element is excluded from the accessibility tree regardless of any other
ARIA attribute on it. dashboard-pagination.mjs's own `.hgh-vpg-sr-only` (visually
hidden but still in the accessibility tree, paired with `aria-current="page"`) is the
correct, complete superset of what organization.mjs's partial decoration did — remove
the superseded code entirely rather than leaving two owners of one element.

Verified by a manual Chromium run against this exact combination (both stylesheets, both
enhancers, real production order, the same fixture tests/mobile/organization.spec.mjs uses):
correctly fails with all 4 pre-cutover symptoms above before this section is applied, passes
cleanly after — reproduction steps recorded in /tmp/fj-vue-pager-20260928-result.md.

